  {
    "episode_id": 81182,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "To understand Buffalo's history is to understand the city's potential. And I was fortunate. I taught history and economics at Buffalo State College. And they let me write and teach a course called the Economic History of Buffalo in Western New York. And in the class, we would chronicle the rise and decline of Buffalo's economy, dating back to the 1901 Pan-American Exposition. In the Penn American Exposition in 1901 was held in Buffalo to showcase to teach the world how electricity and hydroelectricity could change things. In Buffalo at that time was the eighth largest economy in the entire nation. And we look at the development of Delaware Avenue and shape in Lincoln Parkway. Those were the standard homes in Buffalo and those were owned by the captains of industry. But the industry grew up because of the availability of low-cost hydroelectricity. So Buffalo has a fascinating history of look at the architecture. People don't realize this, but Henry Hopson, Richardson, Lewis Sullivan, Louise Bethune, Richard Upjohn, Frank Lloyd Wright, Frederick Olmstead, none of them were from Buffalo. They came to Buffalo. They were the premier architects of their day, but they came to a city because we were a city that exuded a confidence and an economic strength that said to them, you can get your vision turned into something real. You can turn it into something. tangible. You can get your works commission. That's why they all came to Buffalo. So Buffalo's historic foundations are a basis for great community pride, but I would submit to you that I think very few people are familiar with that. People typically are stuck in the moment. They deal with the here and now and they don't understand the depth and the uniqueness of Buffalo's history, which is to be appreciated and celebrated.",
