  {
    "episode_id": 81182,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "That's right. Louise Bethune was the first female architect in the United States. She designed the hotel Lafayette. And you see people like, you know, Rocco Termini, you see people like Nick Sinatra, you see a new generation of developers that are really the visionaries of Buffalo for the 21st century. And there's a lot of people in that group, but it's interesting to note that they're not building a new, they're building new life into what was once here. And that's why these historic restoration projects are so unique. And the good thing is, on the federal level, as a member of Congress, as the vice chair of the House Ways and Means Committee, we set tax policy. And one of the most valuable pieces of legislation that is underappreciated is the federal historic tax credits. And that was responsible for tens of millions of dollars for the restoration of the Richardson complex. And the whole idea is to provide that gap financing. So if you didn't have these tax credits, these historic preservation projects would not be undertaken because they're very complex and they're very challenging. So these tax credits were put in place to promote the redevelopment of places like Buffalo. I mean, no community in America has taken greater advantage of historic tax credits. than Buffalo and new market tax credits. For example, right here at the Buffalo Niagara Medical Campus, the new Science Center was made possible by the availability of new market tax credits.",
