  {
    "episode_id": 81182,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Sure, it's a subset, if you will, of the traditional ideological divide between Democrats and Republicans, and it's finding allies because of the uniqueness of your geography. In the Great Lakes region, we have both Democrats and Republicans who are equally concerned about the environmental integrity of the Great Lakes and the Great Lakes base in the region. So we work together toward the goal of establishing funding priorities, one of the great programs that we have been a great beneficiary of. That being Buffalo and Western New York is the Great Lakes Restoration Initiative. Think about this for a moment. In 1970, the Buffalo River was declared by federal officials as biologically dead, ecologically destroyed, because it was prior to the Clean Water and Clean Air Act, which stopped industry from dumping directly into the river, was called Point Source Pollution. Buffalo color that was making these",
