    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "right? There was a lot of toxic chemicals that were dumped right in the river. So the river was biologically dead. Aquatic life couldn't live there. They were pulling fish. Bottom feeding fish are the Buffalo River that had tumors all over their bodies. So because of programs like the Great Lakes Restoration Initiative, we have removed now 67,000 semi-truckloads of toxic mud from the bottom of the Buffalo River. The same environmental officials at the federal level are now saying that the Buffalo River could be swimmable within the next two years and the fish that are caught there are safe for human consumption within the next decade. That is a massive turnaround. But it's also lent tremendously to the quality of life in Buffalo. We took $11 million of federal money in turned Ohio Street, which was an old broken industrial roadway. There was nothing happening there, nothing along the Buffalo River. and created an award-winning Parkway. Now there's two residential developments, one by Ellicott Development, the other by Severino and his development group that are building luxury apartments on the Buffalo River. If that river was not cleaned up, you couldn't promote luxury living along it.",
