  {
    "episode_id": 81182,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's been helpful. very helpful in reaching out to people to generate support. For example, we're in a little bit of a fight right now about the historic restoration of the central terminal. I think that if that decision goes the wrong way, it will be a generational lost to this community. We will have produced a sub-optimal outcome to an extraordinary opportunity. The central terminal is on the federal registry of historic places, and therefore qualifies for the use of federal and state tax credits, which is about a $11 million piece. Social media has been very helpful in giving us the opportunity to get out there why we're behind it. There are a lot of reasons operationally, historically. I often say that we've been part of Buffalo's Renaissance, as you have in the Buffalo Niagara Medical Campus, but the waterfront and other downtown initiatives. But the Renaissance doesn't mean anything unless it finds its way into the forgotten neighborhoods of Buffalo. And, you know, the skeptics typically prevail, but nothing great ever gets done by the skeptics. And that's true with Buffalo, its history, its current situation and prospectively. But social media has been a big, big piece of generating a lot of information that is accessible and digestible to a lot of people so that they can become activists themselves.",
