    "turn_text": "It's incredible, I forgot to mention that you're right, Matt. And you know, people that are kind of bold, they just jump right out there in traffic, but it's the responsibility of motorists to look for the bicyclists. They own the road, not the motorists. So, it's kind of an interesting difference in thought. Buffalo could and should be like that, particularly given what's happened at the waterfront with a great emphasis on public access, our Olmsted park system, our parkway system. You obviously enjoy shaping and linking parkway. Those places are unique to Buffalo. When you're in a place like Buffalo, when you go to Florida, everything's, nobody's paying taxes in Florida, Because people are drawn by the climate, but Buffalo really has a story to tell and an offering for the rest of the world. I mean, the people that appreciate those old cities of Europe appreciate Buffalo because of the architecture that is so plentiful around us and to think. There have been many times in our history where we've almost lost a lot of that architecture because of just people stuck in the moment. Like, get rid of it, let's build something else. And we still have challenges, you know, the, you know, like the statler building that should be a mainstay and it will be in five years. And it still, it's commercially viable on a limited basis, given its size, but that's going to come along. That's going to come along.",
