    "turn_idx": 56,
    "turn_text": "you know, the gasoline tax. I mean, you know, one thing that we've been able to achieve over the last eight years is better fuel efficiency standards. So people use less gasoline, so they fill up less. And the infrastructure program, if you will, is financed by an 18.4 cents a gallon tax that funds the federal highway program for infrastructure. It's not indexed to inflation. So it hasn't been renewed since 1999. And we've lost a lot of revenues. So finding a new way to finance infrastructure is probably a very, very good thing to do. Tesla will exceed Ford Motor Company in their annual revenues and is coming close to General Motors. So clearly, this is the direction that the nation is moving in. And it's a very good direction for a lot of reasons, including, you know, in the Middle East, there's a population of about 300 million people. It's approximately the same as the United States, right? Take away gasoline and oil. They export an amount equivalent to Finland. They don't produce anything the rest of the world wants. You know, Israel exports computer chips, Syria exports, potato chips. So, if you lessen your reliance for our addiction to their oil by developing alternative energy sources, it's also a national security",
