  {
    "episode_id": 81182,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81182.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 60,
    "turn_text": "Just take this into consideration. One of the most beautiful places in North America is Niagara and the Lake with its natural beauty on Lake Ontario and the gorge. It's like a European city, beautiful wineries with beautiful restaurants in them. It is approximately an hour and 15 minutes from Buffalo. Very few people from Buffalo travel to Niagara and the lake. Toronto, an international city, is 90 miles away. The population of southern Ontario, which is Fort Erie, north to Toronto in the surrounding area, is 12 million people. It is one third of the entire population of Canada. But what's happened over the years because of poor leadership in Buffalo, we have failed to make the connection to Southern Ontario. And it means everything to our economic future. And as somebody who's studied and taught behavioral economics, there's a mindset in Western New York that you avoid the bridge. Why? Because it's not reliable. It's not predictable. Why is that? Well, it was built 90 years ago. with three lanes of traffic. The population in southern Ontario has increased in the last 90 years by 400%. So you've got the busiest northern border crossing between the United States and at the Peace Bridge for commercial vehicles in second for passenger vehicles, three lanes, meaning you're down to one lane, 50% of the time, because they use an alternating lane system. That is short-sighted. That is short-sighted. What you need to do is to build in capacity. by building in more infrastructure to more efficiently move both trucks and passenger cars. And those that will argue that an environmental issue should be arguing for a new Peace Bridge because the thing that triggers asthma in kids is idling diesel engines. The only reason diesel engines are idling is because they're stuck in the Peace Bridge. And I don't care if you have nexus or anything else. It doesn't do you any good when you're on the top of the bridge and you're stuck. But it's also important to the economy of Buffalo West, not only the Buffalo Niagara Medical Campus, but also professional sports franchises. 20 to 25% of the season ticket holders for the sabers and the bills are Southern Ontario. Our ability at the Buffalo Niagara International Airport to attract low-cost carriers like JetBlue and Southwest, 45% of the customer base at the Buffalo Niagara International Airport, is Southern Ontario. Huge tax revenues from the sales tax coming into Erie County is from the Canadian shopper. Estimates are that it's 80% on the weekends. So you drive around the Galleria Mall on a Saturday afternoon and you'll see that the majority of the license plates there are Ontario. So, my point is that we're doing well economically in terms of life quality with one hand tied behind our back. The way you unleash the full potential is to create the capacity that builds in reliability and certainty. So, you remove the mindset from a buffalo in western New York that the bridge is to be avoided. rather than use to experience different things.",
